course: everyday
lesson: 284
title: Facts, opinions, clickbait and saying no to dares
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A fact can be checked («Paris is the capital of France»); an opinion is what someone thinks («Pizza is the best food»). Both are fine, but an opinion should not be passed off as a fact.
explain: False stories are often built to make you very angry or scared, because strong feelings make people share without thinking. «Doctors hate this one trick!» and «this drink cures every illness» are clickbait and myths; real medicines are tested carefully and work for specific problems.
explain: Good checking: use Wikipedia to start and then check the references at the bottom; notice that many accounts repeating the same words may be bots; and wait for trusted news before believing that a famous person has died.
explain: Dares are a kind of pressure trick: they make you feel you must prove something. You don't. Example: friends in Brisbane dare a boy to run across the road before a truck comes. He says «No way» and walks off. A real friend would never want him hurt, and traffic can kill in a second.
explain: Some things are never worth a dare: falls from walls and roofs, traffic, electricity, stealing, being unkind, skipping class, and online «challenges» with choking, holding breath, medicines, fire or detergent pods.
fact: A fact can be checked; an opinion is what someone thinks, and opinions should not be passed off as facts.
fact: When a story makes you very angry or scared, stop and check before sharing; false stories are designed to stir up strong feelings.
fact: «Doctors hate this one trick!» is clickbait selling something; real medical advice comes from doctors and health services.
fact: Use Wikipedia as a start and check its references, because anyone can edit it.
fact: Some accounts are bots or fake accounts that repeat the same thing to make an idea look more popular.
fact: Wait for trusted news before believing or sharing that a famous person has died; celebrity death hoaxes are common.
fact: A «cure for everything» is a myth; real medicines are tested and work for specific problems.
fact: You can always say «No thanks» to a dare; your health matters more than proving anything.
fact: Never jump from high walls or roofs for a dare; falls can break bones or worse.
fact: Never run across a road in front of cars; traffic can kill in a second.
fact: Taking sweets from a shop without paying is stealing; it is against the law and the shop loses out.
fact: Never touch an electric fence or climb an electricity pylon; electricity can kill instantly.
fact: When friends want to leave someone out, invite them in; it takes courage to be kind when others aren't.
fact: Never try online challenges with choking, holding breath, medicines, fire or detergent pods; they have hurt and killed children.
exercise: Which of these is an opinion? | «Pizza is the best food in the world» | «Pizza was first made in Italy» | «Pizza is usually baked in a very hot oven» | «Pizza is often made with cheese»
exercise: What is the difference between a fact and an opinion? | A fact can be checked; an opinion is what someone thinks | A fact is always long; an opinion is short | A fact comes from grown-ups; an opinion comes from children | There is no difference at all
exercise: A post makes you furious about a school that «banned lunch». What should you do first? | Stop and check before sharing | Share it at once to warn others | Send angry messages to the school | Believe it because you feel angry
exercise: Why are many false stories made to scare or anger people? | Strong feelings make people share without thinking | Scary stories are always true | Angry people read more slowly | It makes the stories much cheaper and quicker to write
exercise: An advert says «Doctors hate this one trick to grow taller!». What is it? | Clickbait selling something | Secret advice from top doctors | A new medical discovery | A government health warning
exercise: For a project on volcanoes, how should you use Wikipedia? | Use it to start, and check the references at the bottom | Copy it exactly as the only source | Never look at it at all | Trust it fully because it is popular and millions of people read it
exercise: Hundreds of accounts post the exact same message within minutes. What might they be? | Bots or fake accounts making an idea look popular | Hundreds of friends who agree | A news agency sharing the same facts with everybody at once | A message from the government
exercise: A friend says a famous singer died, but it isn't on any news site. What is wise? | Wait for trusted news before believing or sharing | Post a goodbye message straight away | Tell everyone at school | Believe it because your friend said so and friends don't lie
exercise: A post says a special tea cures every illness. What is true? | A cure for everything is a myth; ask a doctor | It must work, because so many people have shared it this week | Tea cures everything if it is hot | Doctors keep it secret on purpose
exercise: Friends dare you to eat a worm. What can you say? | «No thanks» and change the subject | «OK, just one bite» | «Only if you pay me» | «I'll do it later when nobody's looking»
exercise: Someone dares you to jump off the garage roof. What do you do? | Say no and walk away | Jump onto something soft | Jump if others do it first | Climb up but only look
exercise: Why should you never run across a road for a dare? | Traffic can kill in a second, and no dare is worth that | You might get your new shoes dirty or wet in the road | Drivers will shout at you | It is only dangerous at night
exercise: A friend wants you to take sweets from a shop without paying. What is right? | Say no and leave, because it is stealing | Take only a small one | Let your friend take them, and then just share them with you | Pay for one and take one
exercise: Boys dare each other to touch the electric fence at a farm in Wales. What is true? | Never do it; electricity can kill or injure | It is safe if you touch it quickly and let go straight away | It is safe if you wear trainers | Only cows can be hurt by it
exercise: Your group wants to leave the new girl out of the game. What is kind and brave? | Invite her in or play with her yourself | Leave her out so the rest of the group keeps liking you | Tell her to find another school | Play but ignore her
exercise: A video challenge dares kids to hold their breath until they faint. What is right? | Never try it; such challenges have hurt and killed children | Try it once, but only with a friend watching to catch you | Try it but stop early | It is fine because it is on video
exercise: A friend says «Let's skip PE and hide in the toilets». What should you do? | Stay in class | Skip only half the lesson | Hide but come back before the end | Ask another friend to cover for you
test: Which statement could be checked with a ruler or a book? | «The Nile is one of the longest rivers in the world» | «Rivers are boring» | «Blue is the nicest colour» | «Winter is the worst season of the whole year»
test: A video says «This leaf cures colds, cancer and broken legs!». Why should you doubt it? | Real medicines are tested and work for specific problems | Leaves are too small and too weak to help anyone get better | Videos are never about health | Doctors don't like leaves
test: Your friend in Toronto says «If you don't climb the pylon, you're a baby!». What is the best reply? | «No way — electricity can kill» | «OK, but only halfway» | «I'll do it tomorrow» | «You go first and I'll follow right behind you»
test: A boy says «I'll only be your friend if you nick a chocolate bar». What does this tell you? | He is pressuring you to steal; say no and leave | He is a good friend testing you | Taking small things isn't really stealing, so it's fine | It is fine if the shop is big
test: Ten new accounts with no photos all post «Everyone loves Product X!». What should you think? | They might be bots making it look popular | Everyone really does love Product X, so it must be true | The product must be the best | You should buy it quickly
test: A post says a famous actor has died «this morning», but the actor's own account posted a minute ago. What is likely? | It is a hoax | The actor's account is wrong | Both are true at once | The actor posted after dying
test: Why do people use dares? | To make you feel you must prove something | To help you stay safe | To teach you new skills | To help you make good and sensible choices
test: Your teacher wants a fact, but your notes say «Chocolate ice cream is better than vanilla». What is the problem? | It is an opinion, not a fact | It is a fact about ice cream | It is a fact if most people agree | It is a fact because it is in your notes
test: Everyone is laughing at a boy left alone at lunch. Why does sitting with him matter? | It takes courage, and it matters a lot to him | It makes you more popular | The teacher will notice and give you a sticker for it | It doesn't matter much at all
test: "pizza is the best food" fact or opinion? | Opinion, it's what someone thinks | Fact, everyone agrees | Fact, it's on the menu | Opinion, because it's about food only
test: mates dared me to jump off the garage roof | Say no thanks, you could break bones | Do it if you land on grass | Do it once to prove yourself to them | Jump only if they go first
test: wanna try that breath holding challenge from tiktok | No, those challenges have hurt and killed kids | Yes, if you stop as soon as you start to feel dizzy | Yes, with a friend timing you | Only for 30 seconds at a time
test: post says famous singer died, everyone sharing it | Wait for trusted news, death hoaxes are common | Share it now, so many people can't be wrong | Believe it if it has a sad photo | Post your own message about it first before anyone else
explain: A boy in Ohio took a dare to jump from a garage roof onto a trampoline; he missed the edge and broke both wrists, and spent the summer in casts.
explain: A girl in Sydney shared a scary post saying a famous singer had died; it was false, her friends were upset, and she felt silly when the singer posted the next day.
fact: Swallowing a spoonful of a dry powder like cinnamon for a dare can make you choke and hurt your lungs.
fact: Many children are hurt every year doing dares that looked easy and fun to others.
fact: False stories spread faster than true ones, because they are made to shock or scare.
test: they dared me to eat a whole spoon of cinnamon, its just spice right | No, it can choke you; say no thanks | Yes, it's only a spice | Yes, if you drink water at the same time | Yes, everyone else did it
test: post says famous footballer died, share it?? | Wait for trusted news before sharing | Share it fast so people know | Share it and just say it might be true | Share it if it has many likes
test: video says this tea cures everything | A cure for everything is a myth; ask a doctor | It's true if a doctor in the video says so | It's true if lots of people liked it and commented | It's true if it's natural
test: did u hear a kid jumped off the garage roof for a dare and "broke both legs and his arm", my mum's mate said. lesson? | You can always say no to a dare; falls break bones | Only jump if you land on grass | Dares are fine if everyone's watching and cheering you on | Jump but bend your knees
test: heard on the group chat a pop star "died last night", everyone's posting RIP. what's likely | It may be a hoax; wait for trusted news before believing it | It must be true if lots of people post it, they can't all be wrong | The group chat is always right first | Famous people's deaths are never faked
test: ppl say the girl who wouldn't do the dare is "a scaredy cat, total baby". pass it on? | No, saying no to a dare took courage; don't spread it | Yes, she should've done it | Yes, and dare her again tomorrow in front of everyone at lunch | Yes, it'll make her braver
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between a fact and an opinion? | A fact may be verified, whereas an opinion expresses what someone thinks | An opinion becomes a fact when widely shared | A fact is any statement that has been printed in a newspaper | An opinion may be proven by repetition
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a person should pause before sharing a story that provokes strong anger or fear. | False stories are designed to stir up strong feelings | Strong feelings render a story true | Angry stories cannot be shared lawfully | Frightening stories are always out of date by the time they are read
exam: It is given that a child is challenged to perform a dangerous act for a dare. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | The child may decline, since health matters more than proving anything | The child must comply to retain their friends | The child shall perform it only if the other children are watching | The child shall attempt a lesser version of it
exam: State the correct use of an openly editable encyclopaedia such as Wikipedia. | As a starting point, with its references checked, since anyone may edit it | As a final authority requiring no further verification by the reader | As a source to be avoided entirely | As a source accurate only upon weekdays
